import pf.Html
import ../../../Ui/Heading
import ../../../Ui/Callout
import ../../../Ui/Code
import ../../../Ui/TableWrap
import ../../../Generated/Sources
import ../../../Generated/Paths
import ../../../Ui/PageHero

## The jobs package: what it is for, installing the plugin, a job, queueing one, schedules,
## attempts, time limits and keys, how the workers run jobs, reading the queue, the jobs page, and
## what it doesn't do yet.
meta = {
	title: "Jobs and cron",
	description: "packages/jobs, a plugin for Roc Solid's server: jobs queued in the app's database (Postgres or SQLite) and run by workers until they succeed, schedules, and a page of the queue.",
	toc: [
		{ id: "install", label: "Installing it" },
		{ id: "jobs", label: "A job" },
		{ id: "queue", label: "Queueing one" },
		{ id: "cron", label: "On a schedule" },
		{ id: "options", label: "Attempts, time limits and keys" },
		{ id: "workers", label: "How jobs run" },
		{ id: "listing", label: "Reading the queue" },
		{ id: "page", label: "The jobs page" },
		{ id: "limits", label: "Not yet" },
	],
}

view! = |_|
	<>
		<NoHydration>
			<PageHero
				eyebrow="The ecosystem · jobs and cron"
				title="Work that runs later, and again until it is done"
			>
				Work that shouldn't hold up a request (a welcome mail, a nightly cleanup, a call to a slow service)
				becomes a job: a row in your app's database (Postgres or SQLite), run in the background by workers,
				and tried again, a little later each time, until it succeeds. A schedule queues a job when its time
				comes. Send email from a job (<a href={Paths.ecosystem_email}>packages/email</a>), so an unreachable
				mail server is tried again.
			</PageHero>
			<p>
				<code>packages/jobs</code> is a <a href={"${Paths.ecosystem}#plugins"}>plugin</a> written in Roc, on
				what the server platform gives any plugin: the database and its notifications, background loops, and
				isolated calls (<a href={"${Paths.platform_server}#plugins"}>plugins</a>).
			</p>

			<Heading id="install">Installing it</Heading>
			<p>
				Name the package in your app's header and list its plugin in <code>App.program</code> (this app also
				names <a href={Paths.ecosystem_admin}>packages/admin</a>, for the jobs page):
			</p>
			<Code source={"app [program] { admin: \"…/packages/admin/main.roc\", jobs: \"…/packages/jobs/main.roc\", pf: platform \"…/server/platform/main.roc\" }"} numbers={Bool.False} />
			<Code title={"${Sources.jobs_plugin.file}, in part"} source={Sources.jobs_plugin.rocx} numbers={Bool.False} />
			<p>
				<code>Job.plugin</code> takes one record; only <code>jobs</code> is required:
			</p>
			<TableWrap>
				<table>
					<thead><tr><th>Field</th><th>Default</th><th>What it is</th></tr></thead>
					<tbody>
						<tr><td><code>jobs</code></td><td>(required)</td><td>the kinds of job the workers run: <code>App.jobs</code>, every kind your modules define, found by <code>rocx generate</code> (and every build)</td></tr>
						<tr><td><code>crons</code></td><td>none</td><td>the schedules, each a <code>Cron.every(…)</code> (<a href="#cron">below</a>)</td></tr>
						<tr><td><code>workers</code></td><td>4</td><td>how many jobs run at once</td></tr>
						<tr><td><code>retry_ms</code></td><td>1000</td><td>the wait before a failed job's second attempt, doubled for each attempt after</td></tr>
						<tr><td><code>on_failed!</code></td><td>nothing more</td><td>called with a job that failed for good, after it is logged: its kind, id, attempts and error (for an error tracker, <a href={Paths.ecosystem_posthog}>packages/posthog</a>'s <code>PostHog.job_failed!</code>)</td></tr>
						<tr><td><code>keep_done_ms</code></td><td>a week</td><td>how long a done job is kept after it finished, then swept (<a href="#swept">below</a>)</td></tr>
						<tr><td><code>keep_failed_ms</code></td><td>30 days</td><td>the same for a failed or canceled job</td></tr>
						<tr><td><code>sweep_ms</code></td><td>an hour</td><td>how often finished jobs are swept, besides at start</td></tr>
					</tbody>
				</table>
			</TableWrap>
			<p>
				The plugin adds the queue's table, <code>rocx_jobs</code> (migration <code>jobs/0001_jobs</code>),
				the workers, the schedules' loop and the sweep. At start it checks every schedule and queues again
				the jobs a stop cut short. Without the plugin, queueing a job is an error that says to list it. An
				app on SQLite (the default, <code>data/app.db</code>) always has its database. On Postgres with no
				<code>DATABASE_URL</code>, the server doesn't start, and says why; under <code>--dev</code> and
				<code>--debug</code> it starts with a warning, the plugin runs nothing, and queueing returns the
				database's error.
			</p>

			<Heading id="jobs">A job</Heading>
			<p>
				A kind of job has a name and a function that does the work. The function gets your app's context
				(what its <code>init!</code> made) and the job's input, any type Roc can write as JSON. It answers
				<code>Ok({"{}"})</code> when the work is done, or <code>Err(why)</code>, and the job runs again later.
			</p>
			<Code title={"${Sources.platform_job.file}, in part"} source={Sources.platform_job.rocx} numbers={Bool.False} />
			<p>
				Define kinds at the top of a module's block, as here: <code>rocx generate</code> (and every build)
				finds every <code>Job.define</code> and <code>Job.define_with</code> there and lists them in
				<code>App.jobs</code>.
				A program written by hand lists <code>Job.handler(kind)</code> for each kind instead.
			</p>

			<Heading id="queue">Queueing one</Heading>
			<Code title={"${Sources.platform_enqueue.file}, in part"} source={Sources.platform_enqueue.rocx} numbers={Bool.False} />
			<p>
				<code>{"Job.enqueue!(kind, input, {})"}</code> queues a job to run when a worker is free.
				<code>{"{ when: At(ms) }"}</code> holds it until that moment, in milliseconds since the epoch, as
				<code>Time.now_millis!</code> gives it (a past moment means now). <code>when</code> defaults to
				<code>Now</code>. The input is stored as JSON and read back as the kind's input type. Options have
				defaults: a call names only what it changes, and new options won't break it.
			</p>
			<p>
				Queued inside <code>Db.transaction!</code>, a job is a row in the transaction: it runs only if the
				transaction commits (<a href={"${Paths.platform_database}#transactions"}>transactions</a>).
				<code>Job</code>'s errors are open unions (<code>[JobErr(Str), ..others]</code>), like <code>Db</code>'s.
				So one transaction's body can use <code>?</code> on its statements, its jobs and your own errors
				together, and its caller matches <code>JobErr</code> beside <code>DbErr</code>.
			</p>

			<Heading id="cron">On a schedule</Heading>
			<Code source={"Cron.every(\"0 3 * * *\", nightly, {})       # five fields, UTC: at 03:00 each day\nCron.every(\"* * * * * *\", tick_job, {})    # six begin with seconds: every second"} numbers={Bool.False} />
			<p>
				A schedule in <code>crons</code> queues its job, with its input, each time it comes round. Cron
				syntax: five fields (<code>minute hour day month weekday</code>), or six with seconds first, always
				UTC. A field is <code>*</code>, a number, a range (<code>1-5</code>), a
				step (<code>*/15</code>) or a list of them (<code>1,15,30</code>). Months and weekdays may be named
				(<code>JAN</code>, <code>MON</code>).
			</p>
			<p>
				Each scheduled job is keyed by its schedule and due second, so a restart never queues it twice, and
				a failed run is retried like any job. A schedule the parser (Roc too) can't read, or whose job the
				plugin doesn't run, stops the server at start, saying why.
			</p>

			<Heading id="options">Attempts, time limits and keys</Heading>
			<Code title={"${Sources.platform_job_options.file}, in part"} source={Sources.platform_job_options.rocx} numbers={Bool.False} />
			<ul>
				<li>
					<strong>Attempts and a time limit.</strong> <code>{"Job.define_with(name, { attempts, timeout_ms }, run!)"}</code>
					makes a kind tried <code>attempts</code> times in all, each run within <code>timeout_ms</code>
					milliseconds (0 means no limit). A <code>Job.define</code> kind gets five attempts and no limit.
				</li>
				<li>
					<strong>Keys.</strong> <code>{"Job.enqueue_keyed!(kind, input, { key })"}</code> (and
					<code>when</code>, as for <code>enqueue!</code>) answers <code>Queued</code>, or <code>Exists</code>
					and queues nothing when a job with that key is queued or running. The key is free again once that
					job is done, failed or canceled. A key names what the job is for (<code>reminder:ada</code>), so you
					can find the job by it: <code>Job.cancel!(key)</code> cancels the queued ones (not a running one),
					and <code>{"Job.move!(key, when)"}</code> moves the queued one to another time. Each says how many
					jobs it changed and, inside a transaction, takes effect only if it commits.
				</li>
			</ul>

			<Heading id="workers">How jobs run</Heading>
			<ul>
				<li>
					<strong>Each job is taken once.</strong> Workers are background loops. Each takes the next due job
					(<code>for update skip locked</code> on Postgres, so no two take the same one) and runs it in its
					own call, so a crash fails that job, not the worker. A waiting worker holds no thread.
				</li>
				<li>
					<strong>Woken, not polling.</strong> When its transaction commits, a queued job notifies
					<code>rocx_jobs</code> (Postgres's <code>NOTIFY</code>, or <code>Db.notify!</code> on SQLite), which
					wakes the workers. Otherwise a worker sleeps until the next delayed job or retry is due.
				</li>
				<li>
					<strong>Again, and later.</strong> A failed job (an <code>Err</code>, a crash, or its time limit
					passing) runs again after <code>retry_ms</code>, then twice that, and so on, until its kind's
					attempts are used up. Then it is <code>failed</code>, with its last error, and stays in
					<code>rocx_jobs</code> until you retry it or it is swept. Each failure is logged as a warning; a job
					that failed for good is logged as an error, before <code>on_failed!</code> is called.
				</li>
				<li>
					<strong>Within a time limit.</strong> <code>timeout_ms</code> is a deadline on each run. The first
					effect waiting past it (a database statement, a socket, a request to another server) ends the run
					like a crash, whatever the job does with errors: the statement is cancelled (on SQLite,
					interrupted), its connection closed, the run's memory freed, and the job fails with
					<code>timed out after …</code>. Roc that never waits (a loop with no effect) is stopped too: the
					server interrupts its thread.
				</li>
				<li>
					<strong>Across restarts.</strong> A job running when the server stopped runs again at the next
					start.
				</li>
				<li id="swept">
					<strong>Swept when old.</strong> Finished rows stay for the jobs page and for retries, then are
					deleted: done jobs <code>keep_done_ms</code> after finishing (a week), failed or canceled ones
					<code>keep_failed_ms</code> after (30 days). The sweep runs at start and every <code>sweep_ms</code>
					(an hour), a thousand rows a statement, on Postgres and SQLite. A schedule every ten seconds leaves
					8,640 done jobs a day, so keep them for less (<code>keep_done_ms: 60 * 60 * 1000</code>). Queued and
					running jobs are never swept.
				</li>
				<li>
					<strong>Another plugin's jobs.</strong> A package can bring its own kind of job, as
					<a href={Paths.ecosystem_push}>packages/push</a> does for sending. Its plugin lists
					<code>Job.call(kind)</code> in its calls and <code>Job.adopt!(kind)</code> in its starts, and the
					workers run those jobs too.
				</li>
			</ul>

			<Heading id="listing">Reading the queue</Heading>
			<Code title={"${Sources.platform_job_list.file}, in part"} source={Sources.platform_job_list.rocx} numbers={Bool.False} />
			<p>
				<code>{"Job.list!({ state, kind, before_id, limit })"}</code> reads jobs, newest first; <code>""</code>
				and <code>0</code> mean any. Each is a <code>Job.Row</code>: id, kind, input (as JSON), state
				(<code>queued</code>, <code>running</code>, <code>done</code>, <code>failed</code> or
				<code>canceled</code>), attempts and <code>max_attempts</code>, key, last error, and times in
				milliseconds. Everything else the jobs page uses is there for your pages too:
			</p>
			<ul>
				<li><code>Job.get!(id)</code> reads one job; <code>{"Job.counts!({})"}</code> counts the jobs of each kind in each state.</li>
				<li><code>Job.retry!(id)</code> queues a failed or canceled job again, <code>Job.cancel_id!(id)</code> cancels a queued one, and <code>Job.run_now!(id)</code> makes a queued one due now.</li>
				<li><code>{"Cron.schedules!({})"}</code> lists the schedules and when each comes round next; <code>Cron.run_now!(name)</code> queues a schedule's job now.</li>
				<li><code>{"Job.kinds!({})"}</code> lists the kinds the plugin runs, <code>{"Job.keeps!({})"}</code> says how long it keeps finished jobs, and <code>Job.changes</code> is the live topic where the workers say what they start and finish.</li>
			</ul>

			<Heading id="page">The jobs page</Heading>
			<Code title={"${Sources.platform_jobs_page.file}, in part"} source={"${Sources.platform_admins_only.rocx}\n\n# … in its routes\n${Sources.platform_jobs_page.rocx}"} numbers={Bool.False} />
			<p>
				<a href={Paths.ecosystem_admin}>packages/admin</a> draws a page of the queue on a route of yours, for
				your admins only. Call <code>{"JobsPage.respond!(request, { mount })"}</code> from a
				<code>respond!</code>, which <a href={"${Paths.platform_http}#routes"}>answers every method</a>, in a
				folder whose middleware knows who is an admin. <code>mount</code> is the route's path. The page needs
				the jobs plugin. <code>back</code> is a link to your app's own pages, first in the sidebar (none by
				default). At the sidebar's foot, <em>Back to the app</em> goes to the page of yours you came from, or
				to <code>/</code>.
			</p>
			<Code title="routes/(admin)/admin/jobs/+server.roc" source={"import admin.JobsPage\n\n# routes/(admin)/+middleware.roc guards it, as every route in (admin)/: middleware = Middlewares.admin\nrespond! = |{ request, .. }| JobsPage.respond!(request, { mount: \"/admin/jobs\" })"} numbers={Bool.False} />
			<ul>
				<li>
					<strong>What it shows.</strong> Counts of queued, running, failed, done and canceled jobs; what
					finished in each five minutes of the last hour (done, done after a retry, failed); the latest
					failures, with why; each kind with its attempts, time limit and jobs per state; the schedules, in
					words where it can (<code>0 3 * * *</code> is daily at 03:00), with their next time; and the jobs,
					fifty a page, newest first, with state, attempts, times, key and last error. The sidebar filters by
					state or kind.
				</li>
				<li>
					<strong>One job</strong> (<code>?id=…</code>) opens in a drawer over the list: its input (the JSON,
					laid out), last error, attempts, time limit, key, and timeline from queued to finished.
				</li>
				<li>
					<strong>What it does.</strong> Retry, cancel and run now (for a job or a schedule) are plain forms
					that post to the page and redirect back. An action that can't be done gets a page saying why (409),
					and a post from another site's page is refused (403).
				</li>
				<li>
					<strong>As jobs change</strong>, it refreshes over a stream of events (<code>?events</code>), and its
					times count on (<code>3 s ago</code>); Escape closes the drawer. Links and forms are answered in
					place with View Transitions: the sidebar stays still, the page fades, the drawer slides, and the
					stream stays open (no motion if the reader asks for less). Without script it is the same page with a
					refresh button, and links load whole pages. It follows the light or dark scheme and fits a phone. It
					needs no components or hydration, so it works whatever your pages are made of.
				</li>
			</ul>

			<Heading id="limits">Not yet</Heading>
			<ul>
				<li>
					<strong>A job's log</strong> isn't on the jobs page: what a run logged is in the server's log, with
					its job's id. The options are in <code>packages/jobs/PROPOSALS.md</code>.
				</li>
				<li>
					<strong>Missed schedules</strong> aren't caught up: a time that came round while the server was
					stopped is skipped.
				</li>
			</ul>
			<Callout kind="tip" title="Tested">
				<p>
					The server's tests queue jobs now and later, and in transactions that commit and roll back. They
					fail jobs a few times and for good, crash them, and cut them off at their time limit (on Postgres,
					SQLite, a socket, another server, and in Roc looping forever without waiting), checking nothing is
					left held. They queue, cancel and move jobs by key, list them, use the jobs page's forms behind a
					guard, leave alone a job another app queued, and run a schedule every second.
				</p>
			</Callout>
		</NoHydration>
	</>
